kai: Quick question — trying to have chart set env var from secret if present, else fall back to .Values.env.MY_SERVICE_URL. I keep seeing empty string in pod ENV (not nil) and template outputs empty rather than the fallback. Using envFrom + valueFrom? Here's the snippet I'm using:

kai: ```
env:
  - name: MY_SERVICE_URL
    valueFrom:
      secretKeyRef:
        name: {{ .Values.secrets.serviceSecret | quote }}
        key: url
    value: {{ .Values.env.MY_SERVICE_URL | quote }}
```

sam: That won't work — you can't have both valueFrom and value on same key. valueFrom wins; if secret exists but key is empty you'll get empty. Use a conditional or lookup and default. e.g.:

sam: ```
{{- $s := lookup "v1" "Secret" .Release.Namespace .Values.secrets.serviceSecret }}
- name: MY_SERVICE_URL
  value: {{ if $s }}{{ index $s.data "url" | b64dec }}{{ else }}{{ .Values.env.MY_SERVICE_URL }}{{ end }}
```

marie: Also watch out for empty string vs missing. If secret exists but url == "" you'll still get empty. Prefer the hasKey pattern or check length:

marie: ```
{{- $url := "" }}
{{- if $s }}
  {{- $raw := index $s.data "url" | b64dec }}
  {{- if gt (len $raw) 0 }}
    {{- $url = $raw }}
  {{- end }}
{{- end }}
- name: MY_SERVICE_URL
  value: {{ default .Values.env.MY_SERVICE_URL $url }}
```

jin: If you don't want lookup (expensive) you can render the secret name into values and rely on kube to mount it via envFrom then have an init container to set fallback — but that's more complex. For charts we usually do a template guard:

jin: ```
{{- if and .Values.secrets.serviceSecret (ne (catTemplate "secret.yaml" .Values.secrets.serviceSecret) "") }}
# secret-based env
{{- else }}
# values-based env
{{- end }}
```

